WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:06.020
SIERRA: Hey everyone, so just a quick heads
up that this is our last episode of season

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:12.100
4. We will be taking a break for the holidays
to produce an amazing line up for season 5,

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:19.699
so stay tuned for updates, and have a fabulous
end of your year.

00:00:19.699 --> 00:00:29.010
DW News: Growing American concerns about the
threats posed by China.

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:31.679
Today: It's a new breed of super fast missiles.

00:00:31.679 --> 00:00:33.180
CBN News: It s a hypersonic missile.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.180
Forbes Breaking News: Hypersonic missile.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:35.489
Fox News: Hypersonic weapon system.

00:00:35.489 --> 00:00:39.890
ABC News: Beijing test launched the weapon
this summer, sending it into space and around

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:40.890
the planet.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.340
CNBC Television: We are behind the Chinese,
we are behind the Russians, there s a lot

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:46.160
more work to do on hypersonics.
?ABC News: How would you track it if it were

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:56.670
carrying a nuclear weapon?

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:05.850
SIERRA: Last month, United States General
Mark Milley, the chairman of the U.S. Joint

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:11.200
Chiefs of Staff, held a press conference and
confirmed suspicions that China had tested

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:17.150
a hypersonic missile over the summer. A lot
of the coverage that followed was breathless.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:23.180
In the confusion, it was easy to imagine futuristic,
superfast missiles that could evade our defenses

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:28.409
and strike without warning. Some compared
the test to a sputnik moment , invoking the

00:01:28.409 --> 00:01:34.150
Cold War and its perilous arms races. The
truth is a lot more complex, and many of these

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:39.840
fears may be exaggerated. Still, it was an
exceptional moment, if only because it gave

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:46.110
a new generation a taste of a nuclear danger,
and reminded the world about the fragile system

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:52.579
of mutually assured destruction that has prevented
nuclear war for decades. I m Gabrielle Sierra

00:01:52.579 --> 00:01:59.020
and this is Why It Matters. Today, the truth
about hypersonic missiles.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:08.340
SIERRA: Alright so nuclear weapons are scary
to begin with, and hypersonic missiles with

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:15.080
nuclear warheads sound even scarier. So on
a scale of one to ten, how scared should I

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:16.310
be?

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:23.570
GREGO: So I am not going to tell you how scared
to be. I think a lot of people imagine we

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:29.129
left this nuclear competition behind at the
end of the Cold War. I run into people who

00:02:29.129 --> 00:02:30.840
think that all the time.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:35.769
SIERRA: This is Laura Grego. She is a Stanton
Nuclear Security Fellow at the Laboratory

00:02:35.769 --> 00:02:38.170
for Nuclear Security and Policy at MIT.

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:44.350
GREGO: I'm gen X, I grew up in the 80s and
that was front and center. That was our common

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:50.030
culture was like the Soviets and the US, all
of this stuff. And things have changed significantly.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:55.129
And we've gotten rid of so many nuclear weapons,
but we are entering a dangerous phase again.

00:02:55.129 --> 00:03:02.720
And this is not just bilateral, it's trilateral.
There's the US, Russia and China are all modernizing

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:08.510
their nuclear arsenals and nuclear delivery
systems, recapitalizing, rebuilding, building

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:12.049
new different ways to deliver nuclear weapons.

00:03:12.049 --> 00:03:21.200
SIERRA: As of 2021, there are roughly 13,150
nuclear warheads on Earth, about 90 percent

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:26.740
of which are owned by Russia and the United
States. That sounds like a lot, and it is,

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:31.220
but thanks to disarmament agreements, the
number is way down from a peak of more than

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:37.600
60,000 in 1986. As Laura noted, the threat
has not disappeared, and Russia, the United

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.800
States, and other nuclear players like China
are investing heavily in modernizing their

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:44.040
nuclear arsenals.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:51.629
SIERRA: Ok let s get more specific. Tell me
about hypersonic weapons.

00:03:51.629 --> 00:04:00.019
GREGO: I think the name hypersonic just sounds
really awesome like this is something new,

00:04:00.019 --> 00:04:06.049
and the technology isn't new, the dynamics
aren't even all that new. So the name hypersonic

00:04:06.049 --> 00:04:12.069
missiles, is a little bit of a red herring
because, "Hypersonic," describes the speed

00:04:12.069 --> 00:04:16.910
of something. So hypersonic is five times
the speed of sound. it goes fast, but that's

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:22.960
not special about these missiles. Intercontinental
ballistic missiles go faster than that. For

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:28.960
50 years we've had hypersonic-speed missiles,
so that's not new. When people talk about

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:35.320
hypersonic missiles, really what they're talking
about is missiles that are maneuverable.

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:41.630
SIERRA: To understand why this maneuverability
matters, you have to know how the old technology

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:49.229
works. Meet the Intercontinental Ballistic
Missile, or ICBM, which has dominated nuclear

00:04:49.229 --> 00:04:50.870
strategy for decades.

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:55.419
GREGO: So ballistic missiles go up into the
vacuum of space and come down, just like you

00:04:55.419 --> 00:05:01.040
throw a rock or you throw a baseball. If you
have a ball that you're throwing and you want

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:06.460
it to go as far as possible, your brain is
going to do this calculation for you and pick

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:10.750
the angle. It's sort of a 45 degree angle
and that's how you're going to get the furthest.

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:16.250
SIERRA: In general, ICBMs follow this same
trajectory. They are launched into the atmosphere

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:19.240
with the same type of rockets we use to launch
satellites.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:24.540
GREGO: It's just this big powerful rocket
engine, you know, you know, gets things going

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:31.380
up to a high speed. And normally an intercontinental
ballistic missile will launch, it will burn

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:36.759
for three to five minutes to get going up
to high speeds, like seven or eight kilometers

00:05:36.759 --> 00:05:42.169
per second. And it will send its payload,
which is for intercontinental ballistic missiles,

00:05:42.169 --> 00:05:47.990
it's invariably a nuclear weapon. And it'll
send high up arcing through space. As soon

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:52.210
as a missile burns out, it's not in powered
flight anymore. So it goes up, it'll arch

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:56.931
high up into space, sort of a thousand kilometers
altitude or so, and then come back down on

00:05:56.931 --> 00:06:02.840
the other of the earth. So an ICBM's reason
for being is to deliver something from here

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:07.520
to there in 30 to 40 minutes. Once you launch
it, it goes. And you don't recall it. You

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.639
don't retarget it. It just ends up where you've
targeted it, 30 minutes later.

00:06:11.639 --> 00:06:17.600
SIERRA: Some modern ICBMs have the ability
to alter course a bit on their way back down

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:22.020
to earth, but in general, they always go where
you point them, and you can t change your

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:27.979
mind after launch. They are fast and powerful,
but they are also predictable, and the world

00:06:27.979 --> 00:06:33.620
s advanced militaries have gotten really good
at detecting and tracking them. This is where

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:36.780
hypersonic missiles come in.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:43.050
MOUNT: What most people talk about when they
talk about a hypersonic missile is a hypersonic

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:49.660
glide vehicle. This is a system that is attached
to a normal ballistic missile booster, then

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:55.490
thrown into the atmosphere, it detaches, and
then it glides through the atmosphere maneuvering

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:57.750
in flight at very high speeds.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:03.120
SIERRA: This is Adam Mount. He is a senior
fellow at the Federation of American Scientists

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:08.729
with a focus on U.S. nuclear weapons policy.
So that the difference between a hypersonic

00:07:08.729 --> 00:07:13.960
glide vehicle and a normal ballistic missile
is that a normal ballistic missile is flung

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:19.580
towards its target on a predictable trajectory.
A hypersonic glide vehicle by contrast is

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:23.800
slower because it's having to go through the
atmosphere, but it can also maneuver in the

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:29.789
atmosphere. And so you're not totally sure
when it's fired where it's going to end up.

00:07:29.789 --> 00:07:33.690
SIERRA: Countries around the world have been
developing this technology for decades in

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:38.731
different ways. And while the biggest concerns
are related to nuclear security, countries

00:07:38.731 --> 00:07:44.919
are also developing non-nuclear hypersonic
missiles for use in battle. The basic appeal

00:07:44.919 --> 00:07:49.120
is simple: they re hard to detect, and very
hard to shoot down.

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:54.139
GREGO: The United States missile defenses
and radar systems have been set up to detect

00:07:54.139 --> 00:07:59.090
and defend against ballistic missiles, which
are arcing high up and coming back down. So

00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:02.870
the radars are sort of curated to look for
things that are coming from that direction.

00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:08.099
And so these gliders that are skimming through
the atmosphere literally fly under the radar.

00:08:08.099 --> 00:08:12.680
The radars are looking at high altitudes,
and these will come underneath them. So those

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:16.430
radars, yes, they cannot see these because
they're not really designed to.

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:22.150
SIERRA: However, hypersonic missiles aren
t actually invisible . Some experts think

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:27.620
that they could be spotted with current technology,
for example infrared sensors. But they are

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:33.580
a whole lot sneakier than ICBMs. And that
is enough to put militaries on edge.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:42.529
SIERRA: So can you tell me about the basics
of the recent Chinese weapons tests that prompted

00:08:42.529 --> 00:08:44.290
all this worry?

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:50.550
MOUNT: We're still not sure, given unclassified
information, exactly what China tested or

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:55.910
what they intended to test. It's easy to get
alarmed about headlines about hypersonic missiles

00:08:55.910 --> 00:09:00.670
specifically because it means that we are
vulnerable to our adversaries. It is never

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:06.310
comfortable to be reminded by that. The public
reports suggest that China tested a missile

00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:13.052
system that was capable of, injecting a hypersonic
glide vehicle into low earth orbit. So it

00:09:13.052 --> 00:09:18.829
can orbit the earth more or less indefinitely
and then exit that orbit at a time and place

00:09:18.829 --> 00:09:23.930
of its choosing. This grants it the capability,
for example, to inject itself into an orbit

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:29.940
that goes over the South Pole and then strikes
at the Continental United States from an unexpected

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:33.089
direction, ?
SIERRA: This particular type of technology,

00:09:33.089 --> 00:09:39.899
which puts a hypersonic weapon into orbit,
is called a FOBS, or fractional orbital bombardment

00:09:39.899 --> 00:09:42.029
system, more on that later.

00:09:42.029 --> 00:09:47.540
GREGO: Ballistic missiles between countries
in the Northern Hemisphere, tend to come up

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:52.441
over the North Pole towards each other, from
China to the US, US to China, between all

00:09:52.441 --> 00:09:57.470
three of these countries, they'll come up
northerly. But if you want to get around missile

00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:01.209
defenses, you want to confuse them. One way
you can do it is come from an unexpected direction.

00:10:01.209 --> 00:10:05.470
So hypersonic missiles come from unexpected
directions, on sort of a small scale, making

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:10.990
maneuvers of thousands of kilometers out of
the direction you expect. But if you put something

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:15.100
in orbit and then deorbit it, you can come
around from a completely different direction,

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:19.160
like from the south, you can come up from
the South Pole. So, that strategy of putting

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:24.300
something into orbit and then bringing it
back down when it's over its target is called

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:29.839
orbital bombardment, or if it doesn't complete
one entire orbit, it'll be a fractional orbital

00:10:29.839 --> 00:10:34.000
bombardment system. So what it looked like
China did was put something into orbit and

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:37.920
then deorbit it, and what that something was,
was a hypersonic glide vehicle.

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:43.220
SIERRA: We should note that China s missile
test landed 24 miles off target. And when

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:48.790
asked about the test, Chinese officials stated
it was a routine test of a space vehicle.

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:55.670
Still, the test seemed to combine TWO advanced
missile technologies 1) a hypersonic glide

00:10:55.670 --> 00:11:01.150
vehicle and 2) a Fractional Orbital Bombardment
System, or FOBS. This allows you to put a

00:11:01.150 --> 00:11:06.870
hypersonic glide vehicle into a potentially
indefinite orbit around the earth. FOBS have

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:11.769
a lot of ins and outs. But the basic fear
surrounding them is simple. If your adversary

00:11:11.769 --> 00:11:17.040
has a hypersonic missile in orbit, circling
the earth, you have no way of knowing when

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:20.310
or where it s going to come down.

00:11:20.310 --> 00:11:26.500
GREGO: So that's orbital bombardment, but
why fractional? So fractional means you haven't

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:31.829
quite completed one go round to the Earth.
So each go round of the Earth takes about

00:11:31.829 --> 00:11:37.290
90 minutes at these altitudes. And why do
you have to get out before you complete an

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:44.029
orbit? That is to make sure that you remain
in compliance with the Outer Space Treaty.

00:11:44.029 --> 00:11:50.209
One of the core things in the Outer Space
Treaty is that you are not allowed to place

00:11:50.209 --> 00:11:54.620
nuclear weapons into orbit, or station them
in space in any way. So it doesn't mean you

00:11:54.620 --> 00:11:58.480
can't pop up and come down as an ICBM; that's
not being stationed, according to the Outer

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:04.360
Space Treaty. But putting them in orbit would
be contravening it. So there's a legal analysis

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:10.520
which suggests that as long as you don't complete
one entire orbit, you haven't contravened

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:15.100
your obligations of the Outer Space Treaty.
So it's fractional. So even if you dive down

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:20.480
at, whatever, 89 out of 90 minutes, that legal
analysis says that you still have respected

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:21.540
the Outer Space Treaty.

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:24.870
SIERRA: Wow. That is such a way to skirt around.

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:29.529
GREGO: Yeah. And I think a big thing about
the "no nukes and space" is that it truly

00:12:29.529 --> 00:12:34.779
is meant so that we're not storing nuclear
weapons in space and just having another vector

00:12:34.779 --> 00:12:38.240
of, "When might nuclear weapons rain down
on me?"

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:39.240
SIERRA: Right.

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:42.839
GREGO: So part of this of arming intercontinental
ballistic missiles with nuclear weapons is

00:12:42.839 --> 00:12:47.339
that you can see them coming. And that is
stabilizing. You can not only see them coming,

00:12:47.339 --> 00:12:51.680
but you can see them not coming. Every minute
we know that we're not being attacked with

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:55.350
nuclear weapons because we're looking for
them. But if you have nuclear weapons stationed

00:12:55.350 --> 00:12:59.490
in space and they don't have the same signature
associated with nuclear weapons, that provides

00:12:59.490 --> 00:13:03.230
another other thing we have to worry about
and be concerned about. So I think that that

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:08.470
provision of the Outer Space Treaty, whether
or not a FOBS contravenes it, it provides

00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:13.029
a lot of useful stability.

00:13:13.029 --> 00:13:20.720
SIERRA: Why do countries like Russia or China
want hypersonic missiles?

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:28.180
MOUNT: US adversaries have been pursuing an
across the board effort to defeat US missile

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:33.540
defenses. While the United States has its
missile defenses are aimed mostly at North

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:41.660
Korea. It used to also be against Iran. Russia
and China have been extremely alarmed, very

00:13:41.660 --> 00:13:48.990
public and vocal about the risk that US missile
defenses could sometime someday in the future,

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:55.560
hold their arsenals at risk. The worry is
that the United States with its very capable

00:13:55.560 --> 00:14:01.470
nuclear and conventional strike capabilities,
could conduct a first strike against, Chinese

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:08.440
or Russian capabilities and then use missile
defenses to mop up a retaliatory strike.

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:13.329
SIERRA: When talking about missile defense,
it s very important to distinguish between

00:14:13.329 --> 00:14:19.020
the ability to detect incoming missiles, and
the ability to shoot them down. The U.S. is

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:25.269
very good at the detection part. Sensors on
land, sea and in space are constantly monitoring

00:14:25.269 --> 00:14:30.709
for an attack. If a missile aimed at the U.S.
is detected, the U.S. military would launch

00:14:30.709 --> 00:14:35.399
an interceptor to shoot the missile down.
This is where things get murky.

00:14:35.399 --> 00:14:42.280
GREGO: So the United States fields currently
one system to defend the continental United

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:47.800
States from ICBMs, nuclear-armed ICBMs, it's
called the Ground-based Midcourse Defense

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:52.750
system. It's mostly interceptors based in
Alaska with a few in Vandenberg Air Force

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:58.440
Base in California, but they're meant to detect
an incoming intercontinental ballistic missile

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:05.450
from North Korea and destroy it before it
can get to the US. It does not work very well.

00:15:05.450 --> 00:15:10.060
I mean, it's been decades of effort and tens
of billions of dollars, and it still has not

00:15:10.060 --> 00:15:14.870
yet demonstrated capability even against sort
of the less sophisticated North Korean missiles.

00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:19.810
SIERRA: But these limitations haven t stopped
countries like China and Russia from worrying

00:15:19.810 --> 00:15:21.899
about U.S. missile defense.

00:15:21.899 --> 00:15:26.870
GREGO: China and Russia are sort of looking
decades ahead, and if the United States manages

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:32.480
to make some kind of technological breakthrough,
or it decides to just invest in lots of different

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:36.680
systems for missile defense, they want to
be able to guarantee that their ability to

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:42.190
deliver nuclear weapons to the United States
is secure so that the deterrence relationship,

00:15:42.190 --> 00:15:45.640
mutual assured destruction continues to be
the fact of the day.

00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:51.839
SIERRA: It s worth pausing here, because mutually
assured destruction is a very important concept

00:15:51.839 --> 00:15:57.650
when it comes to nuclear security. It s part
of a broader concept called deterrence.

00:15:57.650 --> 00:16:04.240
SIERRA: So can you give us a quick refresher
on what nuclear deterrence means?

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:10.399
MOUNT: For any nuclear state, the basic requirement
of nuclear deterrence is that if your adversary

00:16:10.399 --> 00:16:16.649
tries to wipe out your nuclear arsenal, you
still have the capability to inflict unacceptable

00:16:16.649 --> 00:16:23.399
damage on them. The terms that we use to describe
this is that if, for example, the United States

00:16:23.399 --> 00:16:29.339
with its extremely capable, conventional and
nuclear strike capabilities attempted to carry

00:16:29.339 --> 00:16:36.459
out a first strike against Russia, China,
or North Korea, they want the ability to still

00:16:36.459 --> 00:16:41.209
be able to retaliate, to still be able to
inflict unacceptable damage on the United

00:16:41.209 --> 00:16:43.800
States and its allies.

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:50.160
SIERRA: The idea here is to make it so dangerous
to launch a nuclear weapon that nobody chooses

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:55.750
to do so. And while it might sound strange,
missile defense systems can make things more

00:16:55.750 --> 00:16:56.750
dangerous.

00:16:56.750 --> 00:17:03.060
MOUNT: If the United States missile defense
system could knock down or defend against

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:08.770
that smaller retaliatory strike package, then
it would undermine this basic requirement

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:15.550
of deterrence. It would mean that the United
States could wipe out an adversary's nuclear

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:21.890
arsenal and face no damage or no consequences
in return. For many years, the US missile

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:28.669
defense system has been relatively small and
aimed primarily at North Korea and also Iran.

00:17:28.669 --> 00:17:37.310
Yet there has always been this wish in US
strategic circles to provide for perfect security,

00:17:37.310 --> 00:17:43.371
to eliminate the vulnerability that the United
States has against its adversaries. So that

00:17:43.371 --> 00:17:50.929
we're relying, not on deterrence, but on defense.
This was Ronald Reagan's wish. And it is a

00:17:50.929 --> 00:17:56.830
wish that remains politically and emotionally
appealing today. It's what explains the US...

00:17:56.830 --> 00:18:04.320
Continued US bipartisan investments in missile
defenses, even though they have had a very

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:11.440
mottled track record. So while US missile
defenses almost certainly do not have the

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:18.820
capability to defend against Russian, China,
North Korean second strike options, those

00:18:18.820 --> 00:18:22.669
adversaries worry that it does. And there
are many in the US Congress and in the United

00:18:22.669 --> 00:18:27.750
States that hope one day that it could, this
is what's driving this new arms race among

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:33.000
US adversaries to defeat, pierce or overwhelm
US missile defenses.

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:38.510
SIERRA: Many experts believe that deterrence
works best when everyone presents an equal

00:18:38.510 --> 00:18:44.230
threat. But if one country pulls ahead, either
in offense or defense, the others must decide

00:18:44.230 --> 00:18:48.070
how to keep up, and this can lead to an arms
race.

00:18:48.070 --> 00:18:53.559
The Chinese test seemed to advance China s
side of the equation, and it came as a surprise

00:18:53.559 --> 00:18:58.250
for people who assumed the United States would
always remain comfortably ahead of the game

00:18:58.250 --> 00:19:00.850
in terms of missile technology.

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:10.200
SIERRA: So I heard this referred to as a Sputnik
moment. So what does that mean? And do you

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:13.000
actually agree with that analogy?

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:20.240
MOUNT: It's difficult to overstate the effect
that seeing Sputnik in the sky had on the

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:26.490
American psyche. It was a moment during the
Cold War where the United States realized

00:19:26.490 --> 00:19:28.090
that it wasn't infallible.

00:19:28.090 --> 00:19:32.710
SIERRA: For those who don t know, Sputnik
was the world s first satellite, and its successful

00:19:32.710 --> 00:19:39.600
launch by the USSR in 1957 shocked the United
States, and spurred an intense technological

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:40.600
competition.

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:45.250
MOUNT: After World War 2, everyday people
realized that the Soviet Union was making

00:19:45.250 --> 00:19:50.990
advances that could match or even exceed the
United States. It's difficult to see the same

00:19:50.990 --> 00:19:57.340
level of impact from these Chinese test. One
reason is we don't in public know exactly

00:19:57.340 --> 00:20:03.220
what was tested or its capabilities, but for
another it's surprising because China already

00:20:03.220 --> 00:20:08.890
has the capability to hold the United States
at risk. It has very capable, Intercontinental

00:20:08.890 --> 00:20:13.929
ballistic missiles at very long ranges. It's
building more of them. The United States has

00:20:13.929 --> 00:20:21.299
been vulnerable to Chinese missiles for decades
now. So it's difficult to see why a new ability

00:20:21.299 --> 00:20:25.220
to do that represents such a major tectonic
change

00:20:25.220 --> 00:20:31.750
MOUNT: In our strategic environment, but it's
also not uncommon for US defense officials

00:20:31.750 --> 00:20:33.520
to think this way.

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:36.180
GREGO:
China's nuclear arsenal is so much smaller

00:20:36.180 --> 00:20:40.870
than the United States and Russia. And I know
you'll have heard that they're certainly growing

00:20:40.870 --> 00:20:46.310
it and making it more sophisticated, but at
present it's much smaller than the US and

00:20:46.310 --> 00:20:52.360
Russia's. So China's problem with the offense,
defense is more acute because then they can

00:20:52.360 --> 00:20:57.250
imagine there might be more reality of the
United States striking them first and not

00:20:57.250 --> 00:21:02.950
being vulnerable. I think that's one driver
of all of these exquisite systems.

00:21:02.950 --> 00:21:09.679
SIERRA: China has roughly 350 nuclear warheads,
and is in the process of building new weapons

00:21:09.679 --> 00:21:15.330
and missile silos. Of course that s just a
fraction of the 5,500 fielded by the United

00:21:15.330 --> 00:21:20.440
States, but it s still a sufficient number
to present a credible threat to the US or

00:21:20.440 --> 00:21:21.800
any other country.

00:21:21.800 --> 00:21:28.250
MOUNT: The United States has been vulnerable
to its adversaries for decades and hypersonic

00:21:28.250 --> 00:21:34.450
missiles don't change that. So this is less
of a story about new capabilities, and it's

00:21:34.450 --> 00:21:40.890
more of a story about our discomfort with
the logic of deterrence. It is more of a story

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:45.250
about how the United States has never been
comfortable being vulnerable to its adversaries.

00:21:45.250 --> 00:21:50.700
It's never been comfortable with the basic
bargain that it could deter an attack on the

00:21:50.700 --> 00:21:55.690
United States. And, so throughout history,
the United States has looked for ways to overcome

00:21:55.690 --> 00:21:56.990
that vulnerability.

00:21:56.990 --> 00:22:02.299
SIERRA: Is the United States developing its
own hypersonic weapons?

00:22:02.299 --> 00:22:08.270
MOUNT: Over the last decade especially, interest
in hypersonics has reached a fever pitch.

00:22:08.270 --> 00:22:13.570
We are deeply concerned about the hypersonic
programs of adversaries, and we're engaged

00:22:13.570 --> 00:22:20.080
in a crash program to develop our own. In
this enormous fervor around hypersonic glide

00:22:20.080 --> 00:22:25.809
vehicles, nobody has stopped to ask the simple
question, what does the United States need

00:22:25.809 --> 00:22:32.340
them for? US adversaries don't have ballistic
missile defense capabilities or cruise missile

00:22:32.340 --> 00:22:38.460
defense capabilities that are as capable as
ours. And so the case for US hypersonic glide

00:22:38.460 --> 00:22:43.850
vehicles is actually much weaker than that
for our adversaries. Yet the amount of money,

00:22:43.850 --> 00:22:49.270
the, the amount of interest, the amount of
bureaucratic politics that is dedicated at

00:22:49.270 --> 00:22:53.820
fielding, these systems as fast as possible
has been overwhelming. It's been irresistible,

00:22:53.820 --> 00:22:59.950
but without an arms control arrangement for
hypersonic missiles, without any sense that

00:22:59.950 --> 00:23:04.190
there would be reciprocal restraint on the
part of our adversaries. There's currently

00:23:04.190 --> 00:23:09.529
not a major reason for the United States to
avoid developing these systems. And so they're

00:23:09.529 --> 00:23:12.570
pursuing these capabilities full tilt.

00:23:12.570 --> 00:23:18.050
GREGO: I'm concerned about overreaction. That
means we got to build more defenses, we got

00:23:18.050 --> 00:23:22.480
to keep up with the Jones's, we have to build
our own of whatever they've got. And it incentivizes

00:23:22.480 --> 00:23:27.220
this cycle, this arms race cycle, if you're
not careful. So if the United States thinks

00:23:27.220 --> 00:23:31.559
missile defense is important and there's a
certain segment that thinks it's important

00:23:31.559 --> 00:23:37.150
and might eventually work, you can't imagine
that it doesn't have any other effect in any

00:23:37.150 --> 00:23:42.520
other country. And to be surprised when they
seek ways to come around it. Surprised when

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:46.900
they seek ways to counter it. I wouldn't overreact,
but I would look at it holistically and say,

00:23:46.900 --> 00:23:52.179
this is a dynamic we need to really address.
It's very hard to admit that you are vulnerable

00:23:52.179 --> 00:23:58.740
to anybody else. And there's a strong incentive
to make sure you can't be. And we're all on

00:23:58.740 --> 00:24:02.860
the boat together. That's the way I see it.
I see it as a very, our security, of course,

00:24:02.860 --> 00:24:07.940
can't come at the total expense of others
and they will work to address any way that

00:24:07.940 --> 00:24:12.480
that happens. So we would be making the same
mistakes we made a generation ago.

00:24:12.480 --> 00:24:19.360
GREGO: And I'm pretty concerned. We re handing
the keys to this complex, excessively dangerous

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:25.000
contraption to a next generation. It feels
very unfair to me. Lots of echoes of the climate

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:30.020
crisis. The same thing, like, boy, we've really
messed this up and here you go, good luck

00:24:30.020 --> 00:24:31.020
kind of thing. I'm really concerned about
making this dangerous, complex thing just

00:24:31.020 --> 00:24:32.020
self-continuing without really seeking all
the ways we can fix it, get rid of it.

00:24:32.020 --> 00:24:37.170
SIERRA: And this is probably the most important
message to take from the fervor around hypersonics.

00:24:37.170 --> 00:24:42.810
Yes, it is concerning that China appears to
have had a successful test. Yes, hypersonic

00:24:42.810 --> 00:24:48.429
glide vehicles do add another layer of risk
to nuclear security. But the real danger has

00:24:48.429 --> 00:24:54.559
been there all along - nuclear weapons themselves.
In the end, hypersonics are just a reminder

00:24:54.559 --> 00:24:59.880
to the world about the fragile system of mutually
assured destruction that has saved us from

00:24:59.880 --> 00:25:01.090
nuclear catastrophe.

00:25:01.090 --> 00:25:07.890
GREGO: I don't think writ large human beings
can coexist with nuclear weapons indefinitely.

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:11.980
We cannot. They will be the end of us. So
we need to always be thinking of ways to make

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:17.570
them less important and less important. And
rather than more important, develop more complicated

00:25:17.570 --> 00:25:23.659
delivery mechanisms to make things more challenging
for each other and to create this big complex

00:25:23.659 --> 00:25:27.380
system, which is prone to mistakes and misunderstandings.
I think that's the wrong direction entirely.

00:25:27.380 --> 00:25:30.020
So that's where I would create my concern.
It isn't hypersonic missiles are going to

00:25:30.020 --> 00:25:36.360
change my everyday existence or I'm ready
for them to rain down on me. It's a symptom

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:41.710
rather than the disease. It's a symptom of
this commitment to nuclear weapons being important

00:25:41.710 --> 00:25:51.940
and recapitalizing. So I would notice the
symptom and pay attention to 

00:25:51.940 --> 00:25:57.929
the disease.

